{"availability":"available","full":"alex-shostka/geo_three_scene","name":"geo_three_scene","description":"Cesium 3D Tiles debugger Demo app with a React Three Fiber overlay: shows active and failed tiles, highlights them on the globe on hover, flies the camera to a tile on click, and looks up GLB metadata. Includes a Core Web Vitals panel (LCP, INP, CLS, FCP, TTFB). Bonus: DOOM runs on a map tile.","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-05-24T13:23:54Z","updated":"2026-10-06T08:54:14Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://geo-three-scene.vercel.app","topics":["3d-tiles","3d-tiles-renderer","cesium","core-web-vitals","digital-twin","digital-twin-application","digital-twins","geospatial","geospatial-data","react","react-three-fiber","threejs","threejs-particle","typescript","web-performance","web-performance-analytics"],"demo":"https://geo-three-scene.vercel.app/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T22:39:22.785Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-11T22:39:22.785Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-11T22:39:22.785Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T22:39:22.785Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["React","Three.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Geo Three Scene is a debugging tool for map tiles. It shows which tiles a Cesium globe loaded, which ones failed, and what data they carry.","The main view is a Cesium globe centered on Amsterdam. It draws OpenStreetMap raster tiles served from the project's own public/tiles folder, not from an external tile server. Each time Cesium loads a tile or fails to load one, the app records it. A small Three.js scene, built with React Three Fiber, shows these records in the corner of the screen."],"features":["Move tiles out of git. Store the map and GLB tiles in Git LFS or object storage to keep the repository small.","Simulate errors in production. Let the deployed build fail chosen tiles too, for example through a ?errorTiles= URL parameter, so the 